WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:11.623
[Federer] Open science is really an umbrella term that&nbsp;includes a lot of different practices that enable&nbsp;people to access the results of science. 

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.720
[Zaringhalam] When I&nbsp;
first learned about open science I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:21.600
best described to me as really resting on three&nbsp;
pillars: you have open data, meaning that the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.160
are made openly available, that they're shared. You&nbsp;
have open source, which means that the underlying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:35.200
analytical tools and software and code are made&nbsp;
openly available, and then you have open access,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.400
which is that publications, that are sort of the&nbsp;synthesis of the data and the results that came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:47.408
after the analysis, are also made openly available&nbsp;for folks to read. [Instructor] 100% accuracy on... 

00:00:47.639 --> 00:00:55.200
[Federer] NLM is really&nbsp;&nbsp;interested in creating expertise and&nbsp;knowledge about data, not just in, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.710
know, the research institution or in libraries, but&nbsp;also preparing the public to be more data savvy.&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.800
[Zaringhalam] Having as many people as possible who are&nbsp;
able to access the data, who are able to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.240
under the hood, make science more rigorous&nbsp;
because you have more folks who are able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:18.560
sort of tap into this data, tap into your code, have&nbsp;
access to the publications that result from it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:25.645
and engage in this consensus building that is so&nbsp;
incredibly important and foundational for science&nbsp;to move forward.

00:01:26.204 --> 00:01:30.720
[Federer] We're in a position that we can&nbsp;
talk with publishers about why open access is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.240
important or talk with them about why they should&nbsp;
be instituting data citation, which would make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:44.080
it easier for us to track data. We have a really&nbsp;
unique position as a medical library, of our sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:50.928
of standing, to really help push forward some of&nbsp;
the practices that will lead to more open science.

00:01:51.338 --> 00:01:53.845
[Upbeat music]

